---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Be Insiculous — Artificial Intelligence Development Studio"
  description="Be Insiculous — an Artificial Intelligence Development Studio: a Rust game engine and six games, and planning software driven by your own AI assistant."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="section-label">artificial intelligence development studio</p>
    <h1>Be Insiculous</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      We develop with agentic workflows and build the tools that use them — a
      Rust game engine and six games, and planning software driven by your own
      AI assistant. Engine, games, and site are all public repositories.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- The art-policy paragraph is quoted from docs/thesis.md, which is where that wording is
       maintained; it matches the sentence on /games/. Change it there first. -->
  <section>
    <h2>The games</h2>
    <p>
      All six are in alpha, and all of them run on <strong>Insiculous 2D</strong> — our own
      2D engine, written in Rust. Each one is two-player, and each has
      escalating <em>chaos modes</em> that bend the rules of the original. They are a run
      at the 20 Games Challenge: playable in the browser today, with desktop builds running
      the same code natively.
    </p>
    <p>
      The games listed on this site are free and use AI art. The games we sell
      carry none — those are made by our own artists, and they ship on Steam
      and the app stores rather than here.
    </p>
    <p>
      The engine pulls double duty: it runs the games, and it is
      a <a href="/engine/">proving ground for agentic coding tools</a> — real
      tasks on a live Rust codebase.
    </p>
    <p>
      Current focus: taking the tools where the games already went. The six
      games run in the browser; the engine’s editor is headed there next — a
      playground where you build with the engine in a tab and take the
      project with you.
    </p>
    <!-- PLACEHOLDER: a paragraph on the art direction (Deion the Insiculous) goes here when it's public. -->
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The planner</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/fortknight/">FortKnight</a> and <a href="/forkknife/">ForkKnife</a> are two
      views of one planner. FortKnight is the schedule: a repeating fourteen-day cycle, a
      page per day, and where your time actually goes by category. ForkKnife is the menu:
      the meals, the dish for each day, and the prep and cooking tasks that come with them.
    </p>
    <p>
      It runs in your browser with no account and no server — your profile
      stays on your device. There is no AI in the app either: if you want help
      from an assistant, you download your files and take them to your own.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cta">
    <a class="button" href="/games/">browse the games →</a>
    <a class="button" href="/fortknight/">open the planner →</a>
    <a class="button secondary" href="/devlog/">read the devlog →</a>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding: 2rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--text-bright);
    max-width: 34em;
  }

  section p {
    max-width: 42em;
  }

  .cta {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  /* .button / .button.secondary now live in src/styles/global.css — the devlog uses them too. */
</style>
